<ContentLayout design="v2" structuredData={__sd} title="External drive says you need to format it: save your files first | StackGeist" description="Windows says “You need to format the disk” on your external drive? Cancel, check Disk Management, rule out hardware failure, and recover files before formatting." active="guides">
<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / External drive says format it</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Storage troubleshooting</p>
<h1 class="page-title">External drive says “You need to format the disk”: what to do before you click Format.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">Windows shows this prompt when it can see the drive but cannot read its file system. The files are often still there. Clicking Format replaces the file system with an empty one, so the first job is to protect the data, then find out why Windows cannot read it.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Click <strong>Cancel</strong>. Try a different cable and a USB port directly on the computer. Open Disk Management and check whether the volume shows as RAW. If the drive clicks, disconnects repeatedly, or holds irreplaceable files, stop using it and consider professional recovery. Otherwise, use recovery software to copy the files to a different drive, and only then format it.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<Figure src="/illustrations/diagram-recover-then-repair.svg" width={960} height={360} alt="Diagram: four steps in order: stop writing, diagnose, recover files to a different drive, then repair the partition table" caption="The safe order for any drive problem: stop writing, diagnose, recover, and only then repair." />
<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Cancel the prompt and stop writing to the drive.</h2>
<p>The prompt reads “You need to format the disk in drive X: before you can use it.” Formatting writes a new, empty file system, which can overwrite the structures recovery tools use to find your files. Cancel it, and don’t save anything new to the drive.</p>
<div class="note">If the prompt appears every time you plug the drive in, keep cancelling it. It won’t go away until the file system is readable again or the drive is formatted.</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Rule out the cable, port, and power.</h2>
<p>A loose or damaged cable can cause read errors that make the file system look corrupt. Before assuming the data is damaged:</p>
<ul class="article-list">
<li>Swap the USB cable, especially if the drive uses an older Micro-B or a worn USB-C cable.</li>
<li>Plug straight into a port on the computer, not a hub, dock, or front-panel port.</li>
<li>If the drive has its own power adapter, make sure it is connected and switched on.</li>
<li>Try another computer if you have one.</li>
</ul></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Check what Disk Management sees.</h2>
<p>Open Disk Management (right-click Start, then <strong>Disk Management</strong>) and find the drive by its size. Don’t click anything that initializes, creates, or formats a volume.</p>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>RAW</strong><p>Windows sees a partition but cannot recognize the file system. This is the usual state behind the format prompt.</p></div>
<div><strong>Unallocated</strong><p>The partition information is missing or damaged. Don’t create a new volume. Microsoft warns that creating a new partition after data loss lowers the chance of recovery.</p></div>
<div><strong>NTFS or exFAT, but no drive letter</strong><p>The file system may be fine. See the <a href="/guides/external-drive-shows-in-disk-management-not-file-explorer/">Disk Management but not File Explorer</a> guide instead.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Look for signs of hardware failure.</h2>
<p>Recovery software reads the whole drive, which puts heavy load on a failing one. Stop and think about a professional recovery service if:</p>
<ul class="article-list">
<li>A hard drive clicks, beeps, or repeatedly spins up and down.</li>
<li>The drive keeps disconnecting and reconnecting.</li>
<li>It fails the same way on different cables, ports, and computers.</li>
<li>It was dropped, or got wet.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the files are irreplaceable, every extra hour a failing drive runs is a risk. If they are not, you can carry on to the next step.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 05</p><h2>Recover the files to a different drive.</h2>
<p>You need a second drive with enough free space for everything you want back. Never save recovered files to the drive you are recovering from.</p>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Windows File Recovery (free)</strong><p>Microsoft’s command-line tool from the Microsoft Store. Use <strong>Extensive</strong> mode for RAW, formatted, or corrupted drives, for example <code>winfr E: D: /extensive /n *.docx</code>. The source and destination must be different drives.</p></div>
<div><strong>PhotoRec (free)</strong><p>Open-source and reads the source drive in read-only mode. It ignores the file system and searches for known file types, so it often works on RAW drives. Recovered files usually lose their original names and folders.</p></div>
<div><strong>Paid recovery software</strong><p>Worth considering when you need folder structure and filenames back, or a preview before recovering. Most let you scan for free so you can check results before paying.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="note note-warn">Chkdsk is a repair tool, not a recovery tool. It needs a file system it can read, so it generally won’t run on a RAW volume. Running repairs before recovery can also change what is left to recover.</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 06</p><h2>Format and test, only after the data is safe.</h2>
<p>Once your files are copied off and checked, format the drive in Disk Management or File Explorer. Then copy some test files to it and back. If the drive goes RAW again, throws errors, or disconnects, retire it for anything important.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>Repair, replace, or send it out?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Keep using it</strong><p>A one-off file system problem after unplugging without ejecting, where the drive formats and tests cleanly afterwards.</p></div>
<div><strong>Replace it</strong><p>It goes RAW again, disconnects, or shows errors after a format. Use it only for data you have copies of, or not at all.</p></div>
<div><strong>Professional recovery</strong><p>Physical symptoms plus files you can’t lose. Don’t run software scans on it first.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">How this guide was made</p><p>These steps are based on Microsoft documentation and the tools’ own documentation, linked below. StackGeist has not run a recovery test on a damaged drive for this guide.</p></section>

